All numbers are small-endian; except lump sizes, which are PDP-endian; and
fields in the DER format, which are big-endian.

Some lumps use DER format. Except where otherwise specified, object
identifiers may be absolute or relative, and if they are relative, are
relative to 2.25.196954517921581521497869385664052876310 (which is the
base OID for Super ZZ Zero). (Some fields are relative to a different OID;
these are noted explicitly where they occur.)

Some of the lumps that use DER format may use overlong length encoding in
some circumstances, even though that is not strictly valid DER format. It
will only encode it that way; when decoding, it will work with either the
overlong encoding or the correct encoding, so you can use an external
program to alter the contents of that lump and it will still work.

With the exception of the .BGM and .FNT lumps, the application types used
in some of the DER lumps are:

* Application type 0 implicit octet string = Can be a length of zero, one,
or two bytes. If zero, then it means transparent. If one, then it is the
Z-index to be drawn at. If two, then the first byte is the Z-index and the
second byte is the colour to use.


=== !SZ0 ===

This lump identifies the file as a Super ZZ Zero file.

This lump is always the first lump (due to starting with a exclamation
mark, since Super ZZ Zero will sort the lumps in order by name), and always
contains exactly seven bytes of data.

The first byte specifies the kind of file:
  0 = Save game
  1 = Unpublished world
  3 = Published world

The other six bytes are a ID which changes whenever the world file is
modified (although it is not globally unique, it is unlikely to be the
same as it was before when modified), in order that the game engine can
check if a save game file does not match the world file. In a save game
file, these six bytes match those in the world file.


=== *.BGM ===

Music files; see music.doc for details.


=== *.BRD ===

The definition of a board. The parts are:
  Header
  Variable properties
  Stats
  Stat grid (only CURRENT.BRD and PREVIOUS.BRD)
  Grid other than stats

The header consists of:

* Encoding flags (2 bytes): Specifies which other fields are present.

* Board flags (2 bytes if bit8 of encoding flags is set, or 1 byte if bit8
of encoding flags is clear)

* Screen number (2 bytes)

* Exits: Two bytes per exit; the low four bits of the encoding flags
specifies which ones are present (the non-present ones will be zero)

* Width/height: If bit4 of encoding flags is set then these are two bytes
each, but if clear then they are one byte each and you must add 1 to each.

* User data (2 bytes if bit5 of encoding flags is set, zero if clear)

* Max stat number (1 byte)

Summary of encoding flags:
  0001 = East exit
  0002 = North exit
  0004 = West exit
  0008 = South exit
  0010 = Large board
  0020 = User data
  0040 = (reserved)
  0080 = Zones
  0100 = Long flag
  0200 = New stat format
  0400 = Layer inversion
  0800 = Variable properties
  1000 = (reserved)
  2000 = (reserved)
  4000 = (reserved)
  8000 = Save game

If bit11 of the encoding flags is set then the variable property list
follows. It starts with one byte being the number of variable properties,
and then each variable property consists of one type byte and then the
data; the length of the data is equal to the low nybble of the type byte.
The possible types are:

* 0x00 = The previous variable property only applies once.

* 0x01 = Set the video mode. Bit0 is set for 80 columns and bit7 is set
for SMZX mode.

* 0x02 = Like 0x01 but also sets the video colour mask.

* 0x04 = Set scroll position; the data is two unsigned 16-bit numbers,
first the X scroll and then the Y scroll, of the board coordinates that
will be placed at the view center.

* 0x11 to 0x18 = Load a font; the data is the lump name excluding the .CHR
or .FNT suffix.

* 0x1F = Replace a single character from the loaded font. The first byte of
the data is the character code, and the rest is the raster data.

* 0x21 to 0x28 = Load a palette; the data is the lump name excluding the
.PAL suffix.

* 0x32 to 0x3F = Window field specification.

* 0x40 = Unload music.

* 0x42 to 0x4B = Load music. The first byte has the low 6-bits specifying
the song number (if all of them are set, then the next two bytes are a
16-bit song number), and the high 2-bits specify the criteria for loading
the music: 0=if no music is already loaded, 1=if the specified .BGM lump
isn't already loaded, 2=if the specified .BGM lump isn't already loaded or
if the current song number is different from the specified song number,
3=always. After the first byte (and the next two for the 16-bit song number
if applicable), will be the lump name excluding the .BGM suffix.

* 0x50 to 0x5E = User-defined. Consists of up to seven 16-bit numbers.

Each stat (the first stat number is stat 1) is as follows:

* Flag (8-bits; only present if bit9 of encoding flags is set)

* Misc1, Misc2, Misc3 (16-bits each)

* Length of text (16-bits) (zero if no text is present)

* Text of this stat

* Speed (8-bits)

* Frame offset (16-bits)

* Mode bits (8-bits)

* If bit5 of the mode bits is set, then one byte being the number of the
zone that the stat is restricted to moving within.

* Number of XY records for this stat (16-bits)

* The data of the XY records

The stat flag specifies which other fields are present, as follows:
  bit0 = Text
  bit1 = Misc1
  bit2 = Misc2
  bit3 = Misc3
  bit4 = Frame offset
  bit5 = Additional XY data
  bit6 = Mode
  bit7 = (reserved)

For each XY record, the first byte is the encoding flag, and then the rest
of the data is according to the encoding flag:
  bit1-bit0 = X coordinate
  bit3-bit2 = Y coordinate
  bit5-bit4 = Instruction pointer
  bit6 = Layer
  bit7 = Delay

* X and Y coordinates: If the encoding flag bits is 3, then a 8-bit or
16-bit number (depending on board width/height; however, the coordinates
are always 16-bits long in CURRENT.BRD). Otherwise, it is the encoding
flag bits number plus the coordinate of the previous XY record minus 1.

* Instruction pointer: Encoding flag bits 0 means 0, 1 means 65535, 2 means
same as previous, and 3 means a specified 16-bit number.

* Layer: Set means read a 8-bit number, clear means same as previous.

* Delay: Set means read a 8-bit number, clear means same as previous.

If the "additional XY data" is present, then another encoding flag follows:
  bit0 = Sensor kind
  bit1 = Sensor color
  bit2 = Sensor parameter
  bit3 = Sensor stat
  bit4 = Frame pointer
  bit5 = Extra value
  bit7-bit6 = (reserved)

The sensor data is 8-bits each, and the frame pointer is 16-bits. The frame
pointer cannot be nonzero if the frame offset for the stat is zero, and it
also cannot exceed the length of the stat's text minus six. The extra value
is also 16-bits, but it can be any number. In all cases, if the data is not
present in this file then the value is zero.

The board grid is encoded in the order:
  Under/kind
  Main/kind
  Over/kind
  Under/color
  Main/color
  Over/color
  Under/parameter
  Main/parameter
  Over/parameter

It is encoded as a sequences of runs; a run is allowed to cross between
layers, but cannot cross between kind/color/parameter.

A run command in the board grid has the high 2-bits specifying where the
data comes from, and then the low 6-bits being the run length; if the
low 6-bits are zero then instead the run length is the next byte plus 127,
but if that next byte is 128 or higher, then also add 128 times the next
byte, and then add 63 more. The possible data sources are:

* 0 = A guess is made. The logic for making guesses is explained below.

* 1 = It is given explicitly, after the run length; one byte per cell.

* 2 = Each cell repeats the value of the previous cell.

* 3 = Each cell repeats the value of the cell whose distance behind this
one is equal to the board width (i.e. a "copy-above" run).

Guesses are made according to the following logic:

* For the kind, the guess is always zero if there is no stat at that
location, or the most recent kind of tile that does have a stat, if this
one does have a stat.

* For the colour and parameter, it guesses the colour or parameter of the
most recent cell of the same kind, or zero if there isn't any.

Bit11 of the encoding flags means that "layer inversion" is in use. After
decoding the board data, any cell where the kind in both the under layer
and main layer are nonzero, will have the two layers exchanged. Layer
inversion also affects stats: If they are exchanged in this way and, one
layer has a stat at those coordinates but the other doesn't, then the
layer field of the corresponding stat XY record is updated.

The zones will be listed next if the bit7 of the encoding flags is set. The
unordered zones are first, and then the ordered zones. Each of these sets
starts with a 16-bit number indicating which zones are present (bit0 for
zone 0, etc), and then each of the zones that is present.

Both unordered and ordered zones start with:

* The flags (16-bits).

* The extra value (16-bits).

After those two fields, the rest is different for unordered zones than
for ordered zones.

A unordered zone then contains a maximum Y value (8-bits if the board
height is less than or equal to 256, or 16-bits otherwise), and then the
zone grid data. The zone grid is stored as contiguous bits starting from
the top-left corner, with the low bit first (there is no alignment). The
number of bytes is calculated by adding one to the maximum Y value, and
then multiplying by the board width, and then dividing by eight and round
down, and then add one. The bytes are stored directly, except that if the
value is 0 or 255 then one more byte follows to specify how many more bytes
will have the same value.

A ordered zone has a 16-bit number of cells and then each run of cells has
the following format:

* bit3-bit0 = One less than the run length.

* bit5-bit4 = The X difference, where 0 means the X coordinate follows
for each cell (16-bits if the board width is greater than 256 or 8-bits
otherwise), 1 means previous minus one, 2 means same as previous, and 3
means previous plus one. (If there is no previous one, assume that the
previous coordinate is 1.)

* bit7-bit6 = The Y difference; it works like the X difference but for Y
coordinates instead of X coordinates.

If the X difference or Y difference mode has both bits clear, then for each
cell in this run, it is follows the X coordinate and then the Y coordinate
(which ever one(s) is applicable).


=== *.CHR ===

A font with 256 characters. This is stored as the raw raster data, starting
with the top scanline of character 0 and ending with the bottom scanline of
character 255. The high bit of each byte corresponds to the left pixel.

This lump must be exactly 3584 bytes long, otherwise it is an error.


=== *.FNT ===

The advanced font format, which uses DER format. This advanced format can
be used for features that .CHR does not support, such as partial fonts,
font sizes other than 8x14, character code mapping, animations, etc (not
all of these features are implemented yet).

This can optionally use the ASN1_IDENTIFIED_DATA to wrap it. Whether or not
it is wrapped, it is a sequence with fields:

* First character code (integer).

* Number of character codes (positive integer).

* Glyph definitions. This is a implicit typed sequence; see below.

* Character code mapping, which is a set of implicit typed sequences and/or
implicit typed octet strings.

* Text direction (enumerated): Currently the only possible value is zero,
meaning left to right, and the first line is at the top.

* Set of animation definitions.

* Case folding, which is a octet string. This is only valid for a font with
character codes 0 to 255 exactly (this might change in future). The format
is: 0x00 to 0x7F means 1 to 128 codes that are case folded to themself,
0x80 to 0xBF means 1 to 64 codes that are case folded to the codes that are
specified by that many following bytes, and 0xC0 to 0xFF means 1 to 64
codes that are case folded by adding a offset (modulo 256) which is the
single next byte that follows.

Any of them may be null, other than the first two; and if there are not
enough fields then the rest will be treated as null.

Currently the only kind of glyph definition is type 0, which contains the
width, and the height, and then a bit string with the raster data (all of
them are packed together even if it is not a multiple of eight). There can
be more glyphs than the number of character codes, in which case the rest
of them can be used for animation, ligatures, etc.

The character code mappings can be (o=octet string, s=sequence):

* Type 0 (s) = One integer being the IBM code page number.

* Type 1 (o) = The ISO 2022 escape sequences for designating the G0 and G1
sets that correspond to this font (both G0 and G1 are optional). Some DOCS
codes might also be valid (but are not required).

* Type 2 (s) = One integer being TRON plane number (0x21 for the first
TRON plane). Optionally second can be a bit string indicating the zones,
where: (0) zone A, (1) zone B, (2) zone C, (3) zone D.

* Type 3 (s) = Mosaics. First is a PC string indicating the kind of mosaics
(e.g. "solid mosaics 2x3"; it can also be blank), and second and third are
integers being the horizontal and vertical number of pieces, and fourth is
a octet string with the character codes of the pieces, indexed by the bits
of the mosaics where the top left is the lowest bit and then the one to the
left of that is the next bit, etc, and the bottom left is the highest bit.
Before the octet string can be a optional bit string, where a bit is clear
if that combination is not available in this font.

* Type 4 (o) = The CHARSET_REGISTRY and CHARSET_ENCODING parts of the XLFD
name of the font, including a hyphen in between. Subsetting is also allowed
but is not expected or required.

* Type 5 (s) = Line drawing. This works like type 3, but the second and
third indicating the divisions of mosaics is not used; and is replaced with
a bit string with eight bits, where a bit is set iff that direction is used
where the directions are in order, starting from pointing to the right and
going counterclockwise in forty-five degrees of each step; and then after
that bit string is a integer telling how many variants of pieces of lines
are present (not including the absence of a line).

* Type 6 (o) = TRON code mapping for a character set with single-byte
characters. The format is like TRON-8, but there is a implied initial <FE>
which is not present, and the control codes have special meanings. The code
<7F> means that the code is valid but not mapped, and <FF> means that the
code is not used at all. Codes <00> to <20> means 1 to 33 more TRON codes
based on the previous one; if the previous is the special codes <7F> or
<FF> then it repeats that but otherwise it is that many next codes in the
"linear" order (e.g. 0x217D, 0x217E, 0x2221, 0x2222, etc). There must be
the same number of codes in here that there are in this font.

(Not all of them are necessarily supported by Super ZZ Zero, but might be
useful in other programs.)

Each animation definition is a sequence with the fields:

* First character code, relative to the first character code of this font.
This is mandatory.

* Number of character codes. This must be at least 1, and the default value
is also 1.

* The animation effect, which is a implicit typed sequence; see below.

* The speed, where 1 is the fastest speed and 255 is the slowest speed. The
default value is 1.

* The initial phase, which can only be present if the speed is present (but
it can be omitted even if the speed is present). The value is from 0 up to
one less than the speed, and the default value is 0.

* The control condition. This can be a explicit typed integer; type 0 means
only if the corresponding bit is clear and type 1 means only if the
corresponding bit is set, and absent or null means always. The integer is
always in the range 0 (for the low bit) to 15 (for the high bit).

The animation effects are:

* Type 0 = Shift the rasters horizontally and/or vertically. Contains two
integers, the first for horizontal shift and second for vertical shift.

* Type 1 = Cycle the character glyphs in the range. This does not contain
any parameters (but it still must be constructed rather than primitive).
The first character glyph in range is replaced with the second one, the
second with the third, etc, and the last one is replaced with what was
the first one.

* Type 2 = The first frame is the original glyph, and the rest of the
frames come from the extra glyphs. The first integer in this sequence is
the second frame glyph number relative to the first extra glyph, and the
second integer is the number of frames (including the first frame). There
can be an optional third item, which is enumerated, which specifies the
oscillation mode, where 0 (the default) means one way, 1 means it reverses
after going one way and the first and last frame are repeated, and 2 is
like 1 but the first and last frame are not repeated.


=== *.HLP ===

A help file.

This has the format of the script file but the set of commands is limited:
  @ : $ ! !-
(normal text is also allowed)

Any ! command can have, if not a label name in this help file and not the
name of another help file, a command number which is the return value. This
may be either decimal, or hexadecimal with $ at front.

Line breaks are LF only. The text is not compressed.

(Support for # commands in help file is not currently implemented; if it
is implemented in future, only a few commands are likely to work.)


=== *.INV ===

Stores inventory lists. Active inventory lists are named as "X" followed
by a digit 0 to 7 (e.g. "X0.INV"). Inactive inventory lists are named as
hexadecimal (like some other lumps do, such as BRD, OVM, SCR, etc).

It starts with the header:

* Size of header, not including this byte (8-bits).

* Number of item slots (16-bits).

* Max heap (32-bits).

* Strength (32-bits).

* Flags (16-bits).

* Cursor (16-bits).

After the header will be the item slots; each one starts with a control
byte indicating the type of the record.

Type 0x00 to 0x3F: There is no additional data for this type of record.
It represents 1 to 64 empty item slots.

Type 0x80 to 0xBF: One item slot, which has the fields:

* Item number (16-bits).

* Quantity: Depends on bit1-bit0 of the control byte, where 0 means the
value is implied (zero if the item number is zero, one otherwise), 1
means 8-bits, 2 means 16-bits, and 3 means 32-bits.

* Flags: If bit2 of the control byte is set, then 16-bits, otherwise the
implied value is zero (no flags are set). The high 8-bits have standard
uses and the low 8-bits are for your own use.

* Ext0: If bit3 of the control byte is set, then 32-bits, otherwise the
implied value is zero.

* Ext1: If bit4 of the control byte is set, then 16-bits, otherwise the
implied value is zero.

* Ext2: If bit5 of the control byte is set, then 16-bits, otherwise the
implied value is zero.

Other types are not currently defined.


=== *.LIB ===

A script library. This has the same format of the script file.


=== *.OVM ===

Overlay memory. Has the same format as the MEMORY lump, but the load
address is not necessarily zero.


=== *.PAL ===

Stores a palette. The format of this lump depends on the lump size:
     16   EGA 16 colours
     48   VGA 16 colours
     64   EGA 64 colours
    128   EGA 128 colours
    192   VGA 64 colours
    384   VGA 128 colours

If either of the two high bits of any byte is set then it is not valid. (It
might be used in future if necessary to make a DER format palette file, in
case the existing functions are insufficient.)

The format for EGA is each colour has one byte, and the high two bits must
be clear. The bits are made "00rgbRGB" where the 6-bit output value will
be bits made "RrRrRr" red, "GgGgGg" green, "BbBbBb" blue.

The format for VGA is each colour has three bytes, in order red, green,
blue, each value being in range zero to sixty-three.


=== *.SCR ===

Screen definitions.

The header is (each coordinate, etc are one byte each):

* Either 0x00 or 0x80; if 0x80 then it has a variable property list, which
comes immediately after the header. See the .BRD format specification for
details about the format of this variable property list.

* Screen flags.

* The border colour.

* Character codes for the four default borders (E, N, W, S); zero means no
default character code for the border.

* Coordinates of the four soft edges (only one byte per edge). These are
the inner edges beyond which it may scroll if the cursor or player moves
beyond these edges, unless doing so would violate the hard edges.

* Coordinates of the four hard edges (only one byte per edge). These are
the inner edges in which the edge of the board or text window must fit; the
outside of the board cannot be beyond them unless the board is too small.

* X and Y coordinates of centre of viewport.

* X and Y coordinates of message text or popup text window title. If the Y
coordinate is 255 then the message text is not displayed.

* Left and right edges of message text.

The flags are:

* bit0 = Set to left align message text, or clear for centre. Left
alignment will be at the X coordinate if possible, moving to the left up
to the left message edge if it is needed in order to fit. Centre alignment
is centred at the specified message X coordinate.

* bit1 = If set, then message text is flashy like in ZZT; if clear, then it
will instead use the colour defined in the screen where the text is written
(which may be multiple colours if they are not all the same in all of the
places where the characters of the message text are written). This is not
meaningful for text windows.

* bit2 = If set, then the EXIT indicators will display the border character
instead of blank.

* bit3 = If set, then the USER indicators will display the border character
instead of blank.

* bit4 = Disable scrolling on this screen if set.

* bit5 = If set, then message text has character zero added on the left and
right edge of the text. If clear, then this extra edge is omitted.

The screen data is compressed with the following commands:

* 0-79 = Copy previous cell 1 to 80 times.

* 80-159 = Copy above cells (i.e. 80 behind) 1 to 80 times.

* 160-239 = Follow by the command byte and colour byte, and then 1 to 80
more bytes being the parameters for that many cells.

* 240 = (Reserved)

* 241-255 (except 247) = A single cell. If bit0 is set then the command
byte follows; if bit1 is set then the colour byte follows; if bit2 is set
then the parameter byte follows. Any omitted bytes are copied from the
previous cell if bit3 is clear, or are zero if bit3 is set.

* 247 = Used for increasing/decreasing values; see below. This means a
sequence of cells that the parameter is increasing or decreasing by one
each time, but the command and colour are same for all of them.

If the code is 247 then it is followed by:

* Flag byte: (128x+7y+z), where x is 0 or 1, y is 0 to 17, and z is 0 to 6.
The x is 0 for decrementing or 1 for incrementing, and y is the number of
cells to be encoded (0 means 3 cells).

* The command byte, if z is 0 or 2 or 5. If 1 or 3, it is copied from the
cell to the left; if 4 or 6, it is copied from the cell above.

* The colour byte, if z is 0 or 1 or 4. If 2 or 3, it is copied from the
cell to the left; if 5 or 6, it is copied from the cell above.

* The parameter byte, which is only present if y>2, otherwise it is the
incremented or decremented value from the cell to the left if z<4 or above
if z>3. If there is more than one cell, the rest are adjusted from the
previous cells in this sequence.

Value of z     0   1   2   3   4   5   6
Command        ?   <   ?   <   ^   ?   ^
Colour         ?   ?   <   <   ?   ^   ^


=== *.SND ===

Wave sounds. The format is: the header, and then the audio data.

* Number of header bytes (8-bits; does not count this one). This must be
4 or else it is an error; later versions might add more, or use a different
format in which case this number will not match the number of header bytes
of any valid formats.

* First flags (8-bits).

* Codec number (low nybble) and second flags (high nybble).

* Sample rate (16-bits). (See the flags below)

The codec numbers are:
  0 = Unsigned 8-bits
  1 = Creative 4-bits to 8-bits ADPCM
  2 = Creative 3-bits to 8-bits ADPCM
  3 = Creative 2-bits to 8-bits ADPCM
  4 = Signed 16-bits
  5 = (unused)
  6 = A-law
  7 = mu-law

The flags are:

* bit0 = Specifies the working of the sample rate, where 1 means hertz and
0 means the Creative Voice format.

* bit1 = Set if the filter should be enabled.

* bit2 = Set for stereo. (Super ZZ Zero does not currently support stereo.)


=== *.USD ===

User-defined structured data. Contains arbitrary data in DER format.


=== *.WIN ===

Window info; this is optionally present for each screen, and is only used
if the screen is used for a text window or item window.

This starts with a header. The first byte is a flag byte (the WF_ flags),
and the second byte is currently unused and must be zero.

After that is the commands. If the high bit is set, then:

* bit4-bit0 = Command code

* bit5 = Set if new parameter follows

* bit6 = Set if new colour follows

If the high bit is clear, then it specifies how many cells will copy the
previous cell. Values higher than eighty are used for special commands,
which are as follows:

* 0x7F = Window colours follows. There is a 16-bit number, where each bit
which is set means one colour byte follows.

See help/editwin.hlp for an explanation of the command codes.

The parameter is usually a character code, but zero can be used to mean
to use the character within the screen at that position instead. In some
cases the parameter can be 255 which also has a special meaning.

The colour can be 0x11 for the primary colour or 0x22 for the secondary
colour; other numbers will use that number as the colour.


=== BRD.NAM ===

This lump starts with the 16-bit highest board number, and then it is
followed by null-terminated strings being the board names (starting with
board zero). (Note that it is possible for a board to have a name even
though there is no such board, or to lack a name even if it exists.)


=== CATALOG.DER ===

This lump contains metadata which is not used by Super ZZ Zero itself,
but may be used by external programs such as catalog services. This lump
is present if and only if the !SZ0 lump specifies that the file is a
published world file. If the world file is modified, this lump will
automatically be deleted and it will be marked as unpublished.

This lump (and the associated "side file") is documented separately.


=== CURRENT.BRD ===

Same as the *.BRD format; used only in save game files, which contains the
current state of the current board.

However, if bit15 of the encoding flags is set (which is only the case for
the CURRENT.BRD lump), then additional data follows after the stats, which
is stat index numbers stored in the board grid. They are in order by the
under layer, main layer, over layer. There is one byte per tile, but if it
is zero then a 16-bit number of how many more zeros follow.


=== DYNASTR ===

This lump is only allowed in a save game file (but it is not mandatory); it
is an error if it is present in a world file.

This lump contains dynamic strings. The first byte is the number of dynamic
strings, and then each dynamic string (1-based numbering) consists of one
byte specifying the length of the text, and then the text.


=== ELEMENT ===

This starts with the 128 bytes for the appearance mapping.

After that will be four animation specifications, which are:

* Mode (1 byte).

* Steps (4 bytes): They will be the offset of appearance mapping, adding to
the offsets specified by the elements.

After that is element definitions. Each element definition consists of:

* Flag byte. The low nybble is the number of bytes of the name length; if
it is zero, then the high nybble is how many definitions to be skipped (in
addition to the current one).

* The name of this element.

* If flag bit7 is set, the major appearance code (one byte).

* If flag bit6 is set, the minor appearance code (one byte).

* If flag bit5 is set, the attributes (four bytes); otherwise it will have
the same attributes as the previous definition.

* If flag bit4 is set, then it has events; there is a 16-bit number with
a bit which is set means an event is present, and then it is followed by
the 16-bit event addresses for each event from 0 to 15.

If neither flag bit7 nor bit6 is set, then the major appearance code will
be AP_PARAM and the minor code will be zero. Otherwise, whichever code is
not set will be zero (if it is the major code, zero is AP_FIXED).


=== GENERAL.DER ===

This lump is optional, and is only needed if any of the features specified
by this lump are used. This contains a ASN.1X sequence with the fields:

* A set of object identifiers specifying features which are mandatory. If
this set contains an unrecognized object identifier, then it is an error.
Any item may also be a sequence of object identifiers; in this case, any
one of them is suitable, where the first one in each sequence is the first
preference over the other ones in case multiple of them are.

* A set of object identifiers specifying features which are used by this
world file but are optional. It is not an error if it contains an
unrecognized object identifier, but it may warn the user about it.

OIDs which are expected in the mandatory and optional sets are:

* ...0.(major).(minor).(patch) for a version number; the (patch) is
optional and should normally only be used in the optional set. The
version number without the (patch) should be used in the mandatory set.
If in future it becomes necessary to emulate bugs of older versions of
Super ZZ Zero, it will use this version number to enable such emulation.

* ...4.0.(height) means that the world uses custom fonts and/or palettes.
The (height) must be fourteen; other numbers are not currently supported.

* ...4.3.0 means that a non-release version is expected, and ...4.3.1 means
that a release version is expected. Published world files normally should
not use non-release versions since the behaviour might not be stable.

All of the further fields are optional. Each one is a implicit typed
sequence with context-specific class; the type number indicates the
meaning of the sequence, and they are:

* 0 = Joystick mapping. There can be up to 32 mappings defined; each is
a sequence, where the first item in the sequence is a PC string with the
button names with semicolons in between, and the second item is a octet
string, with a format described below.

* 1 = Default font/palette. It contains two Visible strings, the first
being the font name and the second being the palette name, in both cases
without the suffix (.CHR, .FNT, or .PAL). Either one may be null to use
the built-in font/palette.

* 2 = Override option. This overrides some of the settings in the user
configuration file if the user configuration file allows its settings to
be overridden by the world file. Each item is a integer (to override it)
or null (to not override it), and if there are not enough then the rest
are assumed to be null. In order, they correspond to: speed, speed_fast,
message_timer, menu_x, menu_y, game_key_repeat (enumerated).

* 3 = Settings for PC sound effects. Contains real numbers (for the
convenience of editing, they are not necessarily in canonical form), in
the order: whole note duration, percussion rate, base note frequency, base
overtone frequency, base undertone frequency. The base note frequency may
also be a sequence of twelve numbers for custom tuning.

The octet strings in the joystick mappings are made as a sequence of the
mappings for levels 0 to 15; there can be less than sixteen mappings in
which case the rest are null. Each byte will be one of:

* 0 to 3 means 1 to 4 null entries.

* 4 to 7 means 2 to 5 copies of the previous non-null entry.

* 8, 9, 13, 16, 17, 24 to 27, and 30 to 126 means a ASCII key code.

* 10 to 12 means the next 1 to 3 bytes are command codes. (See config.doc
for a list of the possible command codes.)

* 128 to 135 means shift while held, to level 1 to 8.

* Adding 128 to a ASCII key code will mean auto fire.


=== GLOBAL ===

An uncompressed global script, which runs on all boards that it is not
suppressed by. This is optional.


=== ITEM.DER ===

Contains item definitions. The format is a sequence of item definitions,
with one-based indexing, each of which is itself a sequence. Any of them
may be null instead of a sequence in case that number is not used. The
fields of the item definition sequence are:

* Item name, which is normally a PC string but may also be a octet string.
(In future, TRON string and Graphic string might also be accepted.)

* Item class, which is a unsigned 7-bit integer.

* Item flags, which is a bit string. (Unlike as is usual for a ASN.1 bit
string, this one has the low bit first.) The first 16-bits are for flags
with a meaning defined by Super ZZ Zero, and the next 16-bits are for
user-defined flags.

The rest of the fields are optional; they must be in the specified order
but any ones can be omitted. Each is a explicit typed value (or a implicit
typed sequence if there is more than one), with the context-specific class
and with the specified type number:

* 0 = A unsigned 32-bit integer which is the weight of the item. If not
specified, then it will be zero.

* 1 = A PC string or octet string being the script for this item. If not
specified, then the script is empty.

* 2 = A unsigned 32-bit integer which is the maximum heap size. The default
value is 0xFFFFFFFF.

* 3 = Element number, which is a unsigned 8-bit integer. If not specified,
then the element number is 255.

* 4 = Description, which is a PC string or octet string (in future, TRON
string and Graphic string might also be accepted).

* 5 = Appearance (same constraints as the name).

* 6 = Price (unsigned 32-bit integer).

* 7 = Ext3 (unsigned 32-bit integer).

* 8 = Ext4 (unsigned 16-bit integer).

* 9 = Ext5 (unsigned 16-bit integer).

* 10 = Octet string with one or two bytes, the first being color and the
second being parameter (both have a default value of zero).

* 11 = Special type, which is enumerated.


=== ITEMRAND ===

This lump contains a sequence of item randomizers, which are used to shuffle
some of the details of item definitions.

Each record is sixteen bytes long, with the first eight bytes for the
criteria and the last eight bytes for the effect.

The criteria consists of:

* Lowest class number (8-bits). The high bit is reserved and must be clear.

* Highest class number (8-bits). The high bit is reserved and must be clear.

* A 16-bit field specifying which user-defined flags of the item definition
must be set to match the criteria.

* A 16-bit field specifying which user-defined flags of the item definition
must be clear to match the criteria.

* A 16-bit field specifying additional criteria:
  bit7 = Appearance name must be not empty

The effect consists of:

* The effect type (8-bits). Currently, this must be zero.

* A 8-bit field specifying things to be affected:
  bit0 = Ext5
  bit1 = Ext4
  bit2 = Ext3
  bit3 = Parameter
  bit4 = Colour
  bit5 = Element
  bit6 = Name
  bit7 = Appearance

* A 16-bit field specifying which user-defined flags are affected.

* Four reserved bytes (which must be zero).


=== MEMORY ===

This lump is mandatory.

The initial state of the program memory (not compressed). If it is too
small then the rest will be zero.


=== MEMORY.ED ===

Like MEMORY but optional and only used in the editor, and there is some
compression applied. For this lump, the starting address is 0x100 instead
of zero.

Each command of the compression code has the high 2-bits specifying the
command code and the low 6-bits being one less than the run length. The
possible command codes are:

* 0 = Homogeneous; all zero

* 1 = Heterogeneous; 8-bit data

* 2 = Heterogeneous; 16-bit data

* 3 = (Reserved)


=== NUMFORM ===

The numeric formats. There are sixteen records, each of which contains:

* The format type (1 byte): One of the NF_ constants in common.h.

* The leading character (1 byte): Used mainly for leading zeros, or for
blank space in some other format types.

* The mark character (1 byte): Not used for all format types. Some types
use it for the nonblank character.

* The division amount (1 byte): Amount to divide the value by before being
converted into the numeric format. (This is mostly useful with meters, but
can be used with any format type.) (This division rounds down for most
formats (although in some cases, it does not round to an integer), but
for meters it will round up.)

If this lump is not present, then only formats 0 and 1 are defined, both of
which are decimal numeric formats, where format 0 means leading spaces and
format 1 means leading zeros.

Format types:

* NF_DECIMAL 'd' = Decimal.

* NF_HEX_UPPER 'X' = Uppercase hexadecimal.

* NF_HEX_LOWER 'x' = Lowercase hexadecimal.

* NF_OCTAL 'o' = Octal.

* NF_COMMA ',' = Display the mark character if this digit position would be
nonblank if it was using a decimal format.

* NF_ROMAN 'R' = Roman numerals. The mark character is the code of the
letter 'A'; you can switch this to 'a' for lowercase. In this case, it is
left-aligned and digit position 0 is the leftmost letter. If the division
amount is 2 then it will display the letter 'S' to mean half. The leading
character is used for all positions if the value is zero.

* NF_LSD_MONEY 'L' = Display currency amount by predecimal English money.
Digit position 0 is for farthings, which is blank unless the division
amount is 2 or 4 in which case it is the number of farthings (from 0 to 3).
Positions 1 and 2 are for pence, 3 and 4 for shillings, and 5 and the rest
are for pounds.

* NF_METER 'm' = Display the mark character if the value is greater than
the digit position, or the leading character otherwise.

* NF_METER_HALF 'h' = Display character code 219 if the value is greater
than twice the digit position, the leading character if less or equal, or
the mark character if it is exactly one less than twice the position.

* NF_METER_EXT 'M' = Like NF_METER but for additional positions.

* NF_METER_HALF_EXT 'H' = Like NF_METER_HALF but for additional positions.

* NF_BINARY 'b' = Display the mark character if a bit is set or the leading
character if a bit is clear.

* NF_BINARY_EXT 'B' = Like NF_BINARY but for additional positions.

* NF_CHARACTER 'c' = The display will be the character whose code is the
low 8-bits of the value. If the digit position is nonzero then it will
correspond to the next 8-bits, etc.

* NF_NONZERO '!' = Display the mark character if the value is nonzero or
the leading character if the value is zero (or if it is less than one after
the division is made).

* NF_BOARD_NAME 'n' = The first half of the board name.

* NF_BOARD_NAME_EXT 'N' = The last half of the board name.


=== OPTION.DER ===

The definition of the special option menu. (This is optional; if it is not
present, then there is no special option menu.)

The top level is a implicit type sequence which is one of the following
things; they are also allowed in other contexts, and the letters indicate
what contexts they are allowed, where T means top level.

[0 IT] A menu. Its fields are:

* The screen number for this menu, which is a integer.

* The sequence of menu items.

A menu may have additional fields, which can be integers and/or sequences;
each sequence represents a page (the first page is zero), and the integers
are the screen numbers for all following pages until another integer.

A menu item is a sequence with fields:

* The key code, which is a Printable string, which must contain exactly
one character, which is a digit 0 to 9 or a uppercase letter A to Z. This
can be omitted for a menu item that is only for display.

* The screen position, which is a integer from 0 to 1999. This may be
omitted if it is not displayed on the screen. Either this one and the
next one must be both present or must be both absent.

* The field width, which is a integer from 0 to 80. This may be omitted
if it is not displayed on the screen.

* The effect of the menu item, which is one of these kind of items, like
those in the top level but the context is I instead of T.

* Optional boolean; if present and true, means to cancel the menu after
doing the effect of this menu item (redundant for a type 4 item).

[1 IT] A special option definition. Its fields are:

* Optional PC string being the option title.

* The option number, which is a integer from 0 to 32767. (This must be
unique for the entire world file.)

* A sequence of OIDs (which can be relative), being the predefined OIDs
for options. If there is more than one, the first one that is usable has
priority. It may be empty.

* A bit string with the flags that are applicable for this option.

* The screen number for editing this option.

* The specification of the type and range. If it is a implicit type 0
sequence, then it contains two integers being minimum and maximum. If
it is a implicit type 1 sequence, then see below.

* The default value, which is a integer.

[2 IT] A help file. The fields (both required) are the screen number (a
integer) and the lump name (a Visible string, without the .HLP suffix).

[3 I] Go to another page within the same menu. The only field is a integer
which is the zero-based page number.

[4 I] No fields; means to cancel the menu.

[5 IT] Sound test; the only field is a Visible string.

A implicit type 1 sequence in the option definition is used for enumerated
options, and it is not a sequence with fields but is a implicit sequence
of sequences each of which has the fields:

* Printable string with a single digit 0 to 9 or uppercase letter A to Z,
being the key code.

* The numeric value of this option, which is a integer.

* The text of this option, which is a PC string.


=== PACK ===

Not currently used. In future, might be used for storing the .asm file and
some editor-only lumps in a compressed pack, for distributing the game when
you want to include the editor-related stuff; it is omitted if you are not
including editor-related stuff. An external program is used to unpack it.


=== PREVIOUS.BRD ===

This has the same format as CURRENT.BRD, and is used when the control flags
to save/restore the board are used.


=== SAVE ===

This lump is only for save game files, but it is no longer used in the
current version; see SAVE.DER instead.


=== SAVE.DER ===

Only used in save game files. See savegame.c for the definition.


=== SAVESYS.DER ===

This is not used by the reference implementation of Super ZZ Zero, but may
be used in save games created by other implementations, and contains things
which are specific to the system.

The format is a key/value list (normally there is only one key/value pair
but it is allowed to have more than one), where the key is a OID and the
value can be anything appropriate according to the OID.


=== SCR.NAM ===

Same format as BRD.NAM but for screens instead of boards. (This is only
used in the editor.)


=== START ===

This lump is mandatory in world files; it is omitted in save game files.

It contains:

* Flags (16-bits).

* Starting board number (16-bits).

* A 16-bit number, where any bit which is set means that the status
variable is initialized to zero and is not included in this lump; if the
bit is clear then the value is specified explicitly (see below).

* Six reserved bytes which must be set to zero.

* Status variables (sixteen, of 32-bits each). One of the above fields has
bits to specify if any of these are omitted.

The flags are:

* bit0 = Clear to automatically pause the game when it starts.

* bit1 = Forty columns mode.

* bit2 = SMZX mode.


=== TEMPLATE.DER ===

This is only used in templates. It contains a sequence of operations to be
applied to the world file when being created from a template (and then this
lump is deleted from the newly created world file). Each operation is a
implicit typed sequence.

* 0 = Contains Visible strings, each of which is the name of a lump to be
deleted from the new world file.


=== TEXT ===

Contains null-terminated global text strings, starting with string 1 (with
no other data). (String 0 is always blank.)


=== TEXT.ED ===

Like TEXT but only used in the editor.

